[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 703,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 466,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108222,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 338,
        "headingId": 466,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>White sponge naevus is an autosomal dominant genodermatosis resulting in leukokeratotic abnormal keratin tonofilament deposition.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-10T13:50:12"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 704,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 467,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108223,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 338,
        "headingId": 467,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-10T13:50:12"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 705,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 468,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108224,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 338,
        "headingId": 468,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>DA02.0 Genetic or developmental disorders involving lips or oral mucosa</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-10T13:50:12"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 706,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 469,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108225,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 338,
        "headingId": 469,
        "headingText": "<p><em>Not recommended:</em> Cannon disease; hereditary leukokeratosis; familial white folded mucosal dysplasia.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2023-05-04T08:47:20"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 707,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 470,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108226,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 338,
        "headingId": 470,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-10T13:50:12"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 708,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 471,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108227,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 338,
        "headingId": 471,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The non-keratinized oral mucosa (buccal, ventral tongue, labial, soft palate, alveolar, and floor of the mouth) is the most common site of involvement {31090139; 26021387; 22352924}. Less frequently affected sites include the nasal, laryngeal, oesophageal, and anogenital mucosae.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-10T13:50:12"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 709,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 472,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108228,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 338,
        "headingId": 472,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The onset of the disorder is typically in infancy or early childhood, but it becomes more prominent during adolescence and persists throughout life. Early lesions appear as non-tender, filmy-white, mildly wrinkled to velvety patches or plaques with a bilateral, symmetrical presentation {30671762; 22352924; 26021387}. During puberty, the adherent plaques are more prominent, with a thickened, spongy, and opaque white surface. The surface architecture varies from mildly irregular and shredded to markedly corrugated. Initially localized, over time they become more diffuse and widespread. When the affected oral mucosa is stretched, the plaques do not disappear. Maceration of the affected intraoral tissue is common along the occlusal plane. Tenderness may occur, especially if the tissue is traumatized.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-10T13:50:12"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 710,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 473,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108229,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 338,
        "headingId": 473,
        "headingText": "<p>The worldwide prevalence is about 0.5 cases per 100&nbsp;000&nbsp;individuals {31090139; 26021387}. White sponge naevus exhibits a high degree of penetrance and variable expressivity with a familial distribution. However, sporadic cases have been documented {29047160}. There is an equal sex distribution and no racial predilection.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2023-05-04T08:50:02"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 711,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 474,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108230,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 338,
        "headingId": 474,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-10T13:50:12"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 712,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 475,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108231,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 338,
        "headingId": 475,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Mutations in <em>KRT4</em> and <em>KRT13</em> have been found {29047160; 31090139; 26021387}. These genes are responsible for the production of keratin proteins that normally combine to form intermediate filaments, but mismatched proteins in white sponge naevus result in tissue fragility. This condition is inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern with reduced penetrance, and some people who carry these mutations do not develop white sponge naevus.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-10T13:50:12"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 719,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 482,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108232,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 338,
        "headingId": 482,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>See <em>Clinical features</em>, above.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-10T13:50:12"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 713,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 476,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108233,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 338,
        "headingId": 476,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The epithelium displays shaggy hyperparakeratosis and acanthosis with vacuolation of the spinous layer {30671762; 17178493}. Within the parakeratin and spinous cell layer, bright eosinophilic perinuclear condensation, representing keratin tonofilaments, is identified. The basement membrane is intact, and inflammation is sparse.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-10T13:50:12"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 714,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 477,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108234,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 338,
        "headingId": 477,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Exfoliative cytology with Pap staining highlights the eosinophilic perinuclear condensation {17178493; 30671762}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-10T13:50:12"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 715,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 478,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108235,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 338,
        "headingId": 478,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-10T13:50:12"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 718,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 481,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108236,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 338,
        "headingId": 481,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> bright eosinophilic perinuclear condensation within the spinous cell layer.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> parakeratosis and acanthosis with vacuolation of the spinous layer.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-10T13:50:12"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 716,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 479,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108237,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 338,
        "headingId": 479,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not applicable</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-10T13:50:12"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 717,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 480,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108238,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 338,
        "headingId": 480,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Episodes of remission and exacerbation are reported, along with intermittent superficial sloughing. Disease progression usually stops after puberty and there are no reports of malignant transformation.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-10T13:50:12"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]